{"id": "0044a7ce-3feb-4ca6-9012-15d391f57810", "text": "Q: Use PS0 and PS1 to display execution time of each bash command A: <p>Admitted, I found this thread looking for examples using $PS0 and $PS1 and I see it has a workable solution for something where unix and linux have a solution since ages: The <code>time</code> command. See <code>man time</code> for details. Your usage could/shoud be:</p>\n<pre><code>$ time echo hi\nhi\n\nreal    0m0.000s\nuser    0m0.000s\nsys     0m0.000s\n$ time sleep 1\n\nreal    0m1.001s\nuser    0m0.001s\nsys     0m0.000s\n$\n</code></pre>\n<p>Here you see at <code>real</code> the time passed over the execution. <code>user</code> is execution time spend in user mode, <code>sys</code> is execution time spend in system (root) mode.</p>\n<p>On the other hand, thanks for the $PS0 and $PS1 example code.</p>\n", "tags": ["bash", "performance", "shell", "ps1"]}
{"id": "4440d6e5-334a-4884-98b2-94e5cc3c17af", "text": "Q: pyqtgraph/PySide6 horizontal gradient flickers when data updates and repeats gradient A: <p>I ended up being up to solve my own issue. The issue here lies in the coordinate mode being set to QGradient.ObjectMode. I needed to change it to QGradient.LogicalMode and then call a function after setting the data to tell it where to start and stop on the x-axis. I'll paste the final working version of the MRE below.</p>\n<pre><code>import sys\nimport random\nfrom PySide6.QtWidgets import QApplication, QMainWindow, QSizePolicy, QHBoxLayout, QHBoxLayout, QSizePolicy, QWidget\nfrom PySide6.QtGui import QColor, QBrush, QGradient, QLinearGradient\nfrom PySide6.QtCore import QTimer\nimport pyqtgraph as pg\n\nclass MainWindow(QMainWindow):\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__()  \n\n        main_widget = QWidget(self)\n        self.setCentralWidget(main_widget)\n\n        plot = SpectrumGraph()\n        layout = QHBoxLayout(main_widget)\n        layout.addWidget(plot)\n\n\n\nclass SpectrumGraph(pg.PlotWidget):\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.setSizePolicy(QSizePolicy.Expanding, QSizePolicy.Expanding)\n        self.init_plot()\n\n        self.wavelengths = list(range(350, 850))\n        self.values = [\n            [0.00153,0.00116,0.00119,0.00138,0.00158,0.00167,0.00176,0.00168,0.00132,0.00095,0.00138,0.00211,0.00271,0.00232,0.00187,0.00167,0.00184,0.00201,0.00216,0.00232,0.00237,0.0019,0.00141,0.00126,0.00139,0.00151,0.00181,0.00211,0.0022,0.00178,0.00134,0.00137,0.00161,0.00181,0.00156,0.0013,0.00116,0.00114,0.00112,0.00136,0.00163,0.00173,0.00127,0.00082,0.00088,0.00125,0.0016,0.00156,0.00154,0.00169,0.00208,0.00248,0.00285,0.00322,0.00346,0.00327,0.00305,0.00297,0.00299,0.00301,0.00289,0.00276,0.00258,0.00235,0.00212,0.00243,0.00279,0.00317,0.00358,0.00397,0.00406,0.00407,0.00431,0.00544,0.00656,0.00731,0.00789,0.00859,0.01029,0.01194,0.01468,0.01838,0.02214,0.02483,0.0